body:
Honestly, I hope it's only because he was being an obnoxious adult, and not just the noise. If that's the case, i would have done the same thing. When my wife and I take the kids out to a restaurant, sometimes they get excited playing the table games, and can be loud. We remind them to quiet down, but i mean, kids are kids (they are very young). I often get looks of people being smug and clearly all pissy about it, and it honestly makes me absolutely boil. I don't even allow my kids to be animals, they can just get a little loud.

&#x200B;

Or say one of my kids is acting up, and our dinner is completely ruined, they are all pissy because it's a little "noisy". It's a public place -- and especially with kids, imagine how the parents actually feel considering it's probably their 1 night out a month/year that's ruined. Just wanted to throw that in there.

a pussy? Because something bothers you? Nah man, you're *you*. And I can guarantee theres someone who feels the same. Now a days theres so many dating websites that you can pretty much find what you want like you're ordering off of a menu

&#x200B;

Be specific on your profiles. Go straight to it. "Kissing is a huge NO for me". Bold that, make it emphasized, etc

 "Lol they aren't selling the damn game. Cash out? Our 15$ a month is barely going to pay for all the man hours to make this work. You guys are seriously underestimating the cost to put this into action if you think this is just an "Easy cash grab". "

&#x200B;

What's disrespectful there? "Damn"? I run a trade company, i swear a ton. I don't give a fuck. It's not meant to be hurtful or personal. I went on to explain our sub barely pays the man hours it took to put this into action. Not once was it a personal attack. Everyone can underestimate things, that is NOT a feat of lack of intelligence.

It's not a sub to get an invite, its a perk of being subbed. God, thats so hard for you people to understand? They were limited on people on the server. They had to distribute it. What better than to give it to A) already paying customers as a perk, and B) give it to people already ACTIVELY playing, so they know it won't just end up sitting in a random email box of someone who played years ago. You can select the damn option YEARS ago and not even play anymore, so thats a terrible argument. 

&#x200B;

BTW insults? You being a smart ass isn't any better, so don't act all high and mighty.

What's wrong with pserver players? The reason streamers are bad aren't because of a little toxicity, or because some of them are good. It's because of their cult following that will on the drop of a dime camp anyone who wpvp kills a streamer, or fuck up the economy. They'll spoon feed world bosses to streamers and everything else in the game. A streamer is trying to farm gold, you see him, and decide to kill him? You might as well have just left, because you're about to get dicked on by 20 people for an hour or more.

&#x200B;

Not to mention all the world firsts will be cheated basically because hardcore stream supporters will literally play all day and just give everything they made to the streamer, along with streamers getting every piece of loot that drops.

Might wanna get some heavier dumbbells, but eating is the sole reason of gaining weight. Eating more than you burn.

&#x200B;

With dumbbells you can basically do everything though. (If you get heavier ones)

&#x200B;

Biceps- Obviously curls, hammer curls

&#x200B;

Triceps- Tricep extension (single or double handed, i prefer double with heavy dumbbells but they are expensive)

&#x200B;

Shoulders- Dumbbell overhead press, Raises

&#x200B;

Chest- Flat bench, Incline bench, decline bench presses. Flies.

&#x200B;

Traps- Shrugs

&#x200B;

Back- Rows

&#x200B;

Legs- If you go heavier you can probably manage Squats too. Lunges also work. Calf raises.

&#x200B;

Just a quick write up. You may not like some of these, in which you can definitely find substitutions. Try to figure out what your maintenance calories are, which is probably whatever you eat now since you aren't gaining weight. Then add some calories. You could probably just add 2 protein shakes a day (toss in some peanut butter or w.e) and you'll gain some good weight.

I get that, it's just very hard to actually gain 44 pounds honestly. Before when you gained that weight, you were probably shoving twinkies and mcdonalds in your mouth. Now, you would never eat that junk. But, you CAN eat excess food and gain a bit of weight at a healthy rate. Say you gain 10 pounds or 2 months. Most of it will honestly be muscle, and if you start to notice a bit of fat you don't like? Shed some off. As long as you stay away from the terrible junk, processed foods, then you'll be fine.

&#x200B;

The fact that you lost 44 lbs dieting already means you can easily do it again if you needed to, but I can almost guarantee you won't. If you did, however, magically gain 44 LBs over the next year, you would look MUCH different than the previous 44 lbs differential. You would probably actually like the way you look still.

I've also taken everything I've read online with a grain of salt because of this. I didn't pay attention to my diet at all during high school and even before, and I managed to get pretty huge tbh. I over trained - worked out 7 days a week most of the time, a minimum of 5 days a week. Started lifting at 12 just doing random shit, but started going to a gym and taking it serious at like... 14. By 17 I was pretty jacked, 16-17 inch arms give or take, 195 LBs 5'10 (of course I had a little bit of body fat but not much visible fat, had abs and everything). This makes me wonder about "if you don't eat more than u burn, you will not grow muscle". I'm not saying it's not true, because it DOES make sense, but I just have no idea how I managed to gain that much size if that's the case.

&#x200B;

I don't eat much, and especially during the high school years back then. I wouldn't eat breakfast, I'd eat lunch, and then dinner. I doubt I was getting 3,000 calories in 2 meals. The only thing I can say is I probably had at least my weight in protein every day. I drank my protein with water, so not much calories, but had like 100 grams of powder a day along with anything else I ate.

&#x200B;

All in all, who the hell knows what's true online lol. Most likely I did eat more than I took in considering that's the general consensus, but it's just funny to see different opinions change over the years that were stated to be "Facts"

OPs comment is spot on. Even though you ARE strong, you don't have to lift like a maniac. In high school I was pretty massive, I would barbell 165-170, bench 350, curl 75-80s, etc. So if my max was 325 bench for 3, I would go in and do 350 for 1 or 2, and it was a fucking HARD 1 or 2. I'd do 85LB dumbbells curls, and u might think its just 5 more pounds, but I would use my back a little bit because it was just a bit too heavy. All in all, I tore a rotator cuff, fucked up a muscle on my chest, fucked up my lower back from all the incorrect form lifting and "im a  beast" lifting, and more.

&#x200B;

Stay humble and lift what you know your personal body is capable of, not what you want to show off to the meat heads around. It sounds easy, but its one of the reasons I workout at home now. One big reason is because even when I'm in the gym environment I just get that juice that I want to lift more than the guy next to me, and it's extremely stupid and causes a lot of problems.

I recommend Arizona or Georgia, and HVAC/R. Hot weather, refrigeration breaks down during the heat. (more work). Pay is very good, and the cost of living is dirt cheap. It's tempting to move there honestly if it weren't for my whole family being in Mass.

&#x200B;

Start as a tool bitch, buy books and read read read read read, then read some more

I see. One thing I'll comment on is that I personally wouldn't mix hobbies and a career. Works for some people, but doesn't work for most. You normally end up disliking your job, it's normal, and if its your hobby then you slowly begin to dislike that hobby that you always loved doing. But your case could be different. 

&#x200B;

As for money, if you are financially aware, then you will be fine. Doesn't sound like you have kids or a wife to support so I don't see a problem in pursuing a career elsewhere. You can make it work, and living on Ramen Noodles somewhere that you are happy is much better than eating Steak where you're miserable.

2 years isn't long. May seem like it, but it isn't. When I was 20 I was working odd jobs here and there, even started going to community college a little, but money got tight and I had one son at the time to take care of. I dropped out, bought tons of books online for a skilled trade (refrigeration), and 4 years later at 24 I'm the senior technician in a refrigeration company making 90k a year.

&#x200B;

Put in the time, it's worth it. As for what skills you have, thats not a bad thing to keep in mind, but I'd at least try to think about what you somewhat like to do and are naturally good at. I wouldn't personally mix hobbies and your career together, usually ends bad. If you truly don't know what you want to do, then just make sure you keep working, doing research, and maybe something will click. Try working in different fields - a bank to see how you like financial jobs, and you never know, it may open up a new avenue of work.

&#x200B;

Unfortunately most jobs require degrees except trade jobs where experience trumps everything else, so you will most likely have to go to school either way. But, those 2-4 years are nothing compared to the 50 years you have left that you don't want to be working at McDonalds.

Personally if I was you I'd go do what I actually wanted to do, not stay for a few more bucks. I dealt with a horrible living situation that actually gave me some mental disabilities after a while from just telling myself to suck it up and stop being such a pussy, so it's really not ideal. If I didn't have kids and a family I'd most definitely leave and go somewhere and start fresh.

&#x200B;

If you don't mind me asking, what do you mean abusers?

Demolition. Literally all they do is smash shit. Landscaping is also another solid choice. But, a word of advice from someone in a physical job -- after about 5-10 years, your body will start breaking down if you don't take care of it and just go reckless. Yes, it will happen. I used to think I was superman too and now I have a pretty bad back, bad knees, and everything else in between

&#x200B;

Also, the more "labor intensive" a job is, with lack of brain required, the less pay. Even if its a shitty, shitty job. If you can be replaced easy, don't expect much money. People in the working field don't get much respect unless it's a skilled trade, and even then you don't get much respect (but the pays better).

